# Line endings, settled here rather than left to whatever each clone happens to have in
# core.autocrlf. Without this file the answer is per-machine, and the first time one of
# them writes a file with CRLF the entire file lands in the diff. That is a several-hundred-line
# change where one line actually moved, and a conflict on every branch that touched it.
#
# text=auto lets git decide text from binary by content and normalizes text to LF in the
# index; eol=lf checks it back out as LF on every platform, so both machines hold the
# same bytes. Bun, Vite, TypeScript and svelte-check all read LF on Windows without
# complaint, so there is nothing on that side asking for the other choice.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells are the exception. cmd.exe parses a .bat as it runs, line by line, and
# LF-only files break it around labels and multi-line blocks. The failure is a script
# that half-runs, not one that refuses to start. .ps1 is not as fragile, but dev.ps1 is
# launched by start.bat and only ever runs on Windows, so it keeps the endings of the
# file that calls it.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Assets, marked rather than left to content-sniffing. text=auto classifies these
# correctly on its own today; the point is that a wrong guess is silent corruption (a font
# that stops rendering, a webp that will not decode) found long after the commit that
# caused it, so the guess is worth removing.
*.webp binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp3  binary
